WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:02.531
Per stirpes versus per capita.

2
00:00:02.706 --> 00:00:06.947
These two beneficiary designations only matter when a beneficiary dies before the

3
00:00:06.967 --> 00:00:10.727
insured, and the exam loves to test the difference.

4
00:00:11.659 --> 00:00:12.545
Here's John.

5
00:00:12.565 --> 00:00:15.129
He's insured for three hundred thousand dollars,

6
00:00:15.154 --> 00:00:20.249
and he names his three children as beneficiaries: Ann, Ben and Cara.

7
00:00:20.796 --> 00:00:25.230
Cara has two children of her own, Dee and Eli.

8
00:00:26.162 --> 00:00:28.332
Now, Cara dies before John does.

9
00:00:28.786 --> 00:00:32.071
When John later dies, what happens to Cara's share?

10
00:00:33.012 --> 00:00:36.610
If John chose per stirpes, which means by the bloodline,

11
00:00:36.739 --> 00:00:39.350
each branch of the family gets an equal share.

12
00:00:39.711 --> 00:00:43.244
Ann gets one hundred thousand, Ben gets one hundred thousand,

13
00:00:43.264 --> 00:00:46.270
and Cara's one hundred thousand passes down to her children.

14
00:00:46.503 --> 00:00:49.044
Dee and Eli each receive fifty thousand.

15
00:00:49.970 --> 00:00:53.255
If John chose per capita, which means by the head,

16
00:00:53.314 --> 00:00:57.098
the money is split equally among the named beneficiaries who are still living.

17
00:00:57.644 --> 00:01:02.396
That's just Ann and Ben, so they each get one hundred fifty thousand dollars,

18
00:01:02.416 --> 00:01:04.935
and Cara's children receive nothing.

19
00:01:05.884 --> 00:01:11.653
So, per stirpes passes a deceased beneficiary's share down to their descendants.

20
00:01:11.828 --> 00:01:15.740
Per capita splits everything among the surviving named beneficiaries.
